= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** Take $E=\{0\}$ and any <non-measurable set> $A\subseteq\mathbb R$. The vertical line $\{0\}\times\mathbb R$ is a planar <Lebesgue null set>: each segment $\{0\}\times[-N,N]$ can be enclosed in rectangles of arbitrarily small area, and the line is a countable union of these segments. Consequently its <subset> $E\times A$ is a <Lebesgue measurable set> of planar measure zero, by <completeness of Lebesgue measure>.

A nonempty factor can therefore have too little <Lebesgue measure> to transmit the other factor's failure of measurability. Its exceptional vertical section at $x=0$ is consistent with <Lebesgue measurable sections> being guaranteed only <almost everywhere>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** Suppose instead that $B=A_1\times A_2$ were a <Lebesgue measurable set> in the plane. The section consequence of <Fubini's theorem> says that there is a <Lebesgue null set> $N\subseteq\mathbb R$ such that
$$
B_x=\{y:(x,y)\in B\}
$$
is a <Lebesgue measurable set> for every $x\notin N$. This version applies to completed <Lebesgue measure>; it can be obtained on bounded rectangles and then extended by a countable exhaustion, so no finite-measure assumption on $B$ is needed.

For every $x\in A_1$ the section is exactly $A_2$, which is not measurable. Thus $A_1\subseteq N$. By <completeness of Lebesgue measure>, every subset of $N$ is measurable, contradicting the hypothesis on $A_1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{A_1\times A_2\text{ is not Lebesgue measurable}.}
$$

= d
{parent=1}
