= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** A <typewriter sequence> sweeps successively through finer dyadic partitions. For $k\geq0$ and $j=0,\ldots,2^k-1$, define the <indicator function>
$$
f_{2^k+j}(x)=\mathbf1_{[j2^{-k},(j+1)2^{-k})}(x),\qquad x\in(0,1).
$$
Its <L2 norm> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\|f_{2^k+j}\|_2^2=2^{-k}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
As $n$ tends to infinity so does its block index $k$, proving <norm convergence> to zero. However, every $x\in(0,1)$ belongs to exactly one of the half-open intervals in each block, so $f_n(x)=1$ infinitely often. For $k\geq1$ there is also an interval in each block that does not contain $x$, so $f_n(x)=0$ infinitely often. Thus there is no <pointwise convergence> at any $x\in(0,1)$, let alone convergence <almost everywhere>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** Replace the dominating representative by $|g|$ if necessary, and take the countable union of the exceptional <Lebesgue null sets> so that every bound holds off a single null set. Since the interval has <Lebesgue measure> one, <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives
$$
\int_0^1|g|^2\,dx\leq\left(\int_0^1|g|^4\,dx\right)^{1/2}\left(\int_0^1 1\,dx\right)^{1/2}<\infty.
$$
Thus $g^2$ is a <Lebesgue integrable function>. The squared functions satisfy $|f_n|^2\to0$ pointwise and $|f_n|^2\leq|g|^2$ <almost everywhere>. The <dominated convergence theorem> therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\|f_n\|_2^2=\int_0^1|f_n|^2\,dx\longrightarrow0.}
$$
This uses the <Lp inclusion on a finite measure space> to turn the assumed fourth-power bound into the second-power domination actually needed.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $M=\|f\|_\infty$, the <essential supremum> of $|f|$. If $M=0$, $f=0$ <almost everywhere> and every <Lp norm> is zero. Suppose $M>0$. Since $|f|\leq M$ <almost everywhere> and $m((0,1))=1$,
$$
\|f\|_p\leq M\qquad(1\leq p<\infty).
$$
For any $a$ with $0<a<M$, the <measurable set> $A_a=\{x\in(0,1):|f(x)|>a\}$ has positive <Lebesgue measure>; otherwise $a$ would be an essential upper bound. Hence
$$
\|f\|_p^p\geq\int_{A_a}|f|^p\,dx\geq a^pm(A_a),\qquad
\|f\|_p\geq a\,m(A_a)^{1/p}.
$$
For fixed $a$, $m(A_a)^{1/p}\to1$. Thus $\liminf_{p\to\infty}\|f\|_p\geq a$. Letting $a\uparrow M$ and combining with the upper bound proves
$$
\boxed{\lim_{p\to\infty}\|f\|_p=\|f\|_\infty.}
$$
The positive-measure superlevel sets explain why the <Lp norms converge to the supremum norm> even when the essential maximum is never attained.
